## Editing this clone — template-owned vs clone-owned

This repo is a **stamped clone of `cs-kernel`**. **Template-owned — edit them in
the kernel template (`cs-kernel/cs/templates/project/…`), never here**, or the
change drifts and `cs update` overwrites it: `CLAUDE.md` · `README.md` ·
`docs/ARCHITECTURE.md` · `docs/projects/README.md` · `.claude/settings.json` ·
`.claude/skills/*` · `.claude/commands/*` · `bin/cs_operator_cron.sh` ·
`.env.example` · `.gitignore` · `requirements.txt`.

**Clone-owned — edit freely here:** `manifest.toml` (the declared per-company
variance, the ONE place values change) · `company/*.md` (the *file* is templated,
its **content is yours** to author) · `campaigns/<name>/` (pack content) ·
`docs/projects/<name>/` (per-project working folders) · `.env` · `ext/`.

Rule of thumb: **behavior/shape → kernel template; company data → clone.** A change
that would help every clone belongs in `cs-kernel` (rule of two + anti-fork charter).

## 0b. The engine is authoritative for what it owns

We do not call it the engine for fun. **Synced mail, entity memory, the task
ledger, reply and auto-reply classification, whether a message needs an answer
— those are the ENGINE's judgements.** Not the kernel's, not this clone's.
Ask it; do not re-derive it.

**When the engine is wrong, fix the engine.** Not a second reading in `cs`, not
a heuristic in `ext/`, not a keyword list in a skill. A patch outside the engine
to compensate for the engine leaves two implementations that disagree, and
nobody able to say which is right — and the wrong one keeps a customer waiting.

The failure this rule is written from: `cs unanswered` decided "answered or not"
from IMAP headers while the engine had already classified the same messages. So
a customer's *"That's fine, thank you very much"* — a thank-you on a finished
job — was raised as open work, and our own autoresponder, landing in Gmail Sent
seventeen seconds after four product questions, counted as a human answer and
hid that customer for seventy days.

**One deliberate exception, and it earned its place with a measurement:** dedup
truth is Gmail's own Sent folder, never the engine archive — the archive was
observed asserting a send (2026-07-28) that Gmail Sent does not contain, and
believing it would mean never writing to somebody we never wrote to. So:
*does this message exist* → Gmail. *What kind of message is it* → the engine.
An exception with evidence behind it is not a licence for the next one; anything
else claiming exception status needs the same standard of proof.

