## 2. CLI (thin transport)

Run every verb as `.venv/bin/python -m cs <verb>`. **The verb reference is
`cs --help`** (one line each; `cs <verb> --help` for its flags and whether it is
dry-run until `--commit`) — a list copied into prose goes stale on the next
kernel pin, so this file does not keep one. Two things `--help` will not say:

- **`cs config` is the answer to "draft or send?"** It prints every
  behaviour-deciding setting, the value actually in force and the file that
  declares it, and flags anything declared twice. Never re-derive that from
  `manifest.toml` and the `.env` chain.
- **`--account` switches the ENGINE profile only** — the Gmail-IMAP verbs refuse
  (exit 2) on any other account: [docs/ARCHITECTURE.md](docs/ARCHITECTURE.md)
  § How it works.

## 3. The two records the mailbox cannot hold

`cs handled <email>` (the operator resolved it off-email — a phone call leaves
nothing in Gmail Sent) and `cs escalated <email>` (nothing is resolved; a named
human has taken the thread over). Both are procedures, and
[`.claude/commands/cs-review.md`](.claude/commands/cs-review.md) § Posture owns
them end to end — the wording that triggers them, resolving "this" to ONE
address, `--commit`, `--undo`. Why they exist:
[docs/ARCHITECTURE.md](docs/ARCHITECTURE.md) § How it works. When they may be
recorded at all is a NEVER — § 5 below.

